ement... Adding a factory method is fine. Actually maybe better to be generic: `int_or_none_min`. Let's name it `positive_int_or_none` with min_value param. Keep simple: `EnvVar.int_or_none_min(32)`.\n\nAlso bool note: not needed here for config.\n\nAlso test may expect that setting the config value via property... fine.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with max_message_chars keyword-only.\n\nValidation: must be int >=32, bool not allowed. Since keyword-only, `def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None)`.\n\nImplement truncation: need to apply to message after %s substitution, before prefix formatting. The cleanest: override `formatMessage`? `super().format(record)` calls `self.formatMessage(record)` which does `record.getMessage()` and applies `record.message = ...`. But we must not modify shared record. So in `format`, compute the truncated message ourselves and build the final string.\n\nApproach: In `format`, avoid `super().format(record)` mutating record? logging.Formatter.format sets `record.message`, and also `record.exc_text`, etc. It sets record.message which is a violation of \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args\" — msg/args untouched; record.message we shouldn't modify either ideally. Actually the requirement mentions msg, args, exc_info, stack_info. Better approach: temporarily set record.message and restore? Safer: implement our own formatting.\n\nSimpler approach: capture original record.message before super().format (super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage()). Then after formatting, we can't easily replace message in the string because format includes exc_info.\n\nBetter: do a controlled approach:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    original_message = record.message\n    record.message = self._truncate(record.getMessage())  # hmm\n```\n\nActually we can do this:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    budgeted = self._apply_budget(record, message)\n    if budgeted is message:  # unchanged\n        return self._format_with_newlines(record, super_format_msg? )\n```\n\nCleanest: temporarily substitute record.message and record.msg? No.\n\nAlternative: replicate logging.Formatter.format partially. Actually there is a trick: set `record.message` to truncated text and call `self._style`... logging.Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): s = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ...\ns += record.stack_info...\n```\n\nformatMessage uses `record.message`. So if we set record.message ourselves before calling super().format, super overrides it. But we can override `formatMessage` to use truncated message:\n\n```python\ndef formatMessage(self, record):\n    return self._style.format(record)\n```\n\nStyle uses record.message. So set record.message trimmed, but restore original after. Approach:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    orig = getattr(record, \"message\", None)  # can't use getattr default trick\n    had = \"message\" in record.__dict__\n    orig = record.__dict__.get(\"message\")\n    try:\n        # truncation applies to getMessage() result which logging will recompute in super().format\n        ...\n```\n\nsuper().format does `record.message = record.getMessage()` and then formatMessage. So we need to intercept between. Override a helper: define our own `formatMessage`? Hmm, but formatMessage receives record with record.message set — at that point we can truncate:\n\n```python\ndef formatMessage(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    message = record.message\n    truncated = self._truncate(message)\n    if truncated is message:\n        return super().formatMessage(record)\n    saved = record.message\n    record.message = truncated\n    try:\n        return super().formatMessage(record)\n    finally:\n        record.message = saved\n```\n\nHmm but formatMessage uses record.message via style; but style's format may also use record attributes. The `%` style: `self._fmt % record.__dict__`. record.message = truncated → non-destructive since we restore. But wait, super().format() after formatMessage appends exc_info and stack_info separately, so those aren't truncated. \n\nBut the message could also be affected by formatTime? No.\n\nAlso the multiline alignment uses `record.message` in `format`: `msg.find(record.message)` — after super().format returns, record.message is the truncated one (we restored it inside formatMessage to... hmm). Wait: we save record.message inside formatMessage as the truncated? Let's trace: formatMessage is called with record.message = full message (set by super().format). We set record.message = truncated, call super().formatMessage → returns formatted line, then restore record.message = full message. Then back in `format`, `record.message` is full message, but msg contains truncated text. Then `msg.find(record.message)` returns -1 → prefix = msg[: msg.find(...)] = msg[:-1]... that breaks. Note original code guards with `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` — find gives -1 only if not found; then prefix would be wrong. So we must handle.\n\nBetter: compute prefix differently. Change the alignment code to use formatMessage output rather than record.message? We can compute prefix as msg up to the start of the message body: use `msg.find(displayed_message)` where displayed_message is what we inserted. Let's restructure: keep a helper `_format_message(record)` that returns the (possibly truncated) message text, and set `self._last_displayed`? Not thread-safe-ish but formatter per handler.\n\nCleaner: do the whole format ourselves without super().format? Risky but manageable. Let's instead override format as:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message_full = ... no\n```\n\nAlternative approach: compute truncated message and temporarily swap record.message and record.msg? record.msg is used by getMessage(); but super().format already called getMessage. Actually we could do:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    shown = self._truncate(message)\n    if shown is message:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n    else:\n        original_msg, original_message = record.msg, record.__dict__.get(\"message\")\n        record.msg = shown  # getMessage returns record.msg when no args\n        record.args = ()\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            record.msg = original_msg\n            record.args = original_args\n```\n\nThat mutates msg/args temporarily — the requirement says不能改写共享原记录; temporary mutation then restore... risky if another handler formats concurrently. Better avoid.\n\nApproach: implement formatMessage override plus adjust alignment to use the message actually formatted. I can store it in an instance attribute set within formatMessage? Not thread safe across concurrent records? Formatters may be used concurrently. Use a thread-local? Overkill.\n\nAlternative simpler: don't rely on record.message for prefix; compute prefix length as len(msg) - len(displayed) - len(tail). Hmm, msg = prefix + displayed (+ exc info/stack). So prefix = msg[: len(msg) - len(tailrest)]. Actually simpler: prefix = msg[: msg.find(displayed_msg)] where displayed_msg is the truncated message. We can compute displayed message again in format (deterministic given record). So:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    displayed = self._displayed_message(record)  # truncated\n    if \"\\n\" in msg and displayed:\n        idx = msg.find(displayed)\n        ...\n```\n\nBut `_displayed_message` recomputes getMessage(); that's fine and cheap enough.\n\nHmm, but careful: record.message stays full — fine.\n\nWait, another subtlety: formatMessage override truncation — but also `record.message` used by nothing else? Fine.\n\nActually simplest: in format, compute truncated and pass it through a temporary attribute on the formatter? Let's just do:\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(self, message: str, record) -> str\n```\n\nand in format:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    shown = self._truncate(message, record)\n    if shown is message:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n    else:\n        # temporarily present truncated text to the base formatter\n        saved_message = record.message\n        # override formatMessage via instance attr\n        self._pending_display = shown\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            self._pending_display = None\n```\n\nMeh. Let's just do the formatMessage approach + a small helper to remember, using an instance attribute set in formatMessage. Actually the truly clean way: rewrite format without super():\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    shown = self._truncate(message, record)\n    record.message = shown  # base class also sets record.message; we set to shown\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, setting record.message — requirement only forbids msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. `record.message` is a derived cache set by logging.Formatter.format. But the \"完整处理器\" downstream would do `record.message = record.getMessage()` again anyway (base Formatter.format always recomputes), so setting record.message is harmless. Indeed base Formatter.format starts with `record.message = record.getMessage()`. So each formatter call recomputes from msg/args. So we can freely set record.message.\n\nSo implement format ourselves mirroring base but with truncated message:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = self._truncate(record.getMessage(), record)\n    s = self.formatMessage(record)  # uses record.message\n    ...\n```\n\nBut base Formatter.format also handles time, exc_text, stack_info. I can just replicate:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        record.message = self._budgeted_message(record)\n        s = super().format(record)\n```\n\nWait super().format recomputes record.message = record.getMessage(), overwriting. Hmm.\n\nSo replicate base format logic? Actually I could avoid: use super().format but pass through a wrapper... no.\n\nReplicating base Formatter.format (Python 3.11+) is ~20 lines:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        record.message = self._budget_message(record)\n        if self.usesTime():\n            record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n        s = self.formatMessage(record)\n        if record.exc_info:\n            if not record.exc_text:\n                record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\n        if record.exc_text:\n            if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                s = s + \"\\n\"\n            s = s + record.exc_text\n        if record.stack_info:\n            if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                s = s + \"\\n\"\n            s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)\n        # multiline prefix\n        ...\n```\n\nformatMessage uses self._style.format(record) which uses record.__dict__ (message included). Good. This preserves behavior: usesTime, formatTime override, formatException, formatStack.\n\nBut careful: base `format` clears `record.exc_text`? In 3.11: after using exc_text... Actually 3.11 code:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        record.message = record.getMessage()\n        if self.usesTime():\n            record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n        s = self.formatMessage(record)\n        if record.exc_info:\n            # Cache the traceback text to avoid converting it multiple times\n            if not record.exc_text:\n                record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\n        if record.exc_text:\n            if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                s = s + \"\\n\"\n            s = s + record.exc_text\n        if record.stack_info:\n            if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n                s = s + \"\\n\"\n            s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)\n        return s\n```\n\nYes. This is stable across 3.8–3.13 basically. Good.\n\nAlso note multiline alignment in original: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` and prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]. With our record.message = truncated, find works. But wait: if truncated message contains \"\\n\"? Multi-line handling. Keep as is.\n\nHmm but careful: the original message: prefix computed from msg.find(record.message) — with truncated message it still works. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: setting record.message modifies shared record attribute. Downstream full handler (base Formatter.format) recomputes record.message from getMessage(), so full text preserved. Good.\n\nNow truncation function:\n\n```python\n_OMISSION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    remaining = max_chars - len(_OMISSION_MARKER)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMISSION_MARKER + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nFor budget 32: remaining = 32-15 = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example ✓.\n\nNote negative remaining if max_chars small — but min 32 ensures remaining >= 17. Fine.\n\nkeep_full_message: `if getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True: return message`. Requirement: exactly True. `record.keep_full_message is True` works with getattr.\n\nValidation in __init__:\n\n```python\nif max_message_chars is not None:\n    if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nMessage should include something helpful; not required to include var name here? \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\" — just ValueError.\n\nStore sel